the first key to monitoring gke cost  optimization  is ensuring that cluster administrators  have an understanding of how much of  their cluster resources  are actually used by workloads in the  cluster  with this data they can take steps to  ensure that fewer cluster resources  are idle or wasted let's take a look at  how we can utilize a monitoring system  around this  [Music]  as we explored in the previous video the  concept of bin packing is focused on  ensuring that most of our cluster  resources  are allocated to cluster workloads  cluster administrators want to achieve  efficient bin packing to ensure that a  cluster is not over provisioned  monitoring systems will be able to tell  cluster admins if this is the case  by letting them know how much of a  cluster's allocatable resources  are actually consumed by workload  requests  there are three main areas we want to  monitor to ensure that we are not  stranding resources across our cluster  cpu memory and the amount of pods we run  on each node cpu and memory are well  known concepts in kubernetes so let's  start with how we should monitor how  well  they're actually allocated in a cluster  up to this point we have discussed  monitoring at the aggregate cluster  level  with per cluster monitoring we are  getting a high level summary of how well  we are doing with cluster bin packing  however the larger the cluster and the  more diverse the machine types  the more important it becomes to monitor  at a more granular level  that will help cluster administrators  take action  monitoring at the more granular per node  pool level can help us take this action  this monitoring can help identify target  areas that cluster administrators  can improve cluster bin packing for  example if we are isolating a set of  workloads to their own node pool  that is not correctly sized to fit such  workloads  underutilization may not appear to be a  problem at the aggregate cluster level  but it could very well be a problem  within that node pool  for both cluster level and node pool  level monitoring we should collect these  four metrics around cpu and memory  allocatable resources which references  total cpu or memory available  from the cluster or node pool this value  will always be slightly lesser than the  total resources from the cluster vms  due to critical system components that  have to run on each node  requested resources which references  total cpu or memory requested by pods  running  in the cluster or node pool recommended  resources  which reference recommended cpu or  memory requests from the platform  which can come from components such as  vertical pod auto scaler  and finally used resources which  references  the actual cpu or memory utilization  from pods in the cluster or node pool  in this example dashboard for monitoring  cpu  we can see how these four metrics fall  together over a specified time window  this graph tells us for instance that  there is opportunity for cost  optimization  due to used cores falling well below the  requested core count  by workloads in the cluster however when  looking at this example dashboard for  monitoring memory you get a much better  picture of what is going on  workloads are requesting and using most  of the memory we have  available but barely utilizing the cpu  available  with this information we can consider  the types of machines we are running in  our cluster  or in our node pool can we provision vms  with the same amount of memory but with  less cpu  with the appropriate data at hand we can  now begin this exercise of answering  this question  to practice cost optimization the third  area we should monitor to ensure  efficient bin packing  is pods per node in gke  users can set the number of pods allowed  to run on a node  to a smaller than the default value  which is 110 pods  this is commonly used as a mechanism for  preserving ip addresses  since each pod in a cluster or in a node  pool gets its own ip  this feature in gke is called flexible  pod sider  if you set a small enough number of pods  on a node there's a chance that you  could schedule the maximum pods  per node without using a majority of the  allocatable cpu  and memory on that node this is also  possible  even with the default value of 110 pods  per node  should you have very large nodes with  smaller workload requests  thus it's important to monitor when you  brush up against the maximum pods on a  given node  especially if you are attempting to  achieve ip address preservation  monitoring across these key areas will  help cluster admins continually optimize  their cluster infrastructure  in a data-driven fashion this is  especially important as workloads scale  up and down  and when new tenants on board into the  cluster  be sure to check out the paper we  published on how to set up this  monitoring system  linked in the description below setting  this monitoring up  will lay the groundwork for the cluster  admins to work with cluster tenants  to ensure that they are doing the work  to perform app right sizing  we'll cover this in the next video so  stay tuned  you
 

